  </head>

  <body>
    <h1 align="center">Sgt. Mayor</h1>
    <hr size=2>

    <p>Sgt. Mayor is an english cardgame - or at least I learned it from two
      english people (or actually from Ver&oacute;nica (who is not exactly
      english, but learned it herselve from those two english. But who cares
      ...)).</p>

    <p>The game is played by only 3 persons, who each get 17 carts. The
      missing 52th can be exchanged from the person owing the 2 of spades.</p>

    <p>The players have to get 9, 5 and 3 ticks respectively. It starts the
      player needing to get 9 ticks by selecting the colour of the trumps
      (should this be spades, can the player which previously exchanged the
      two of spades change this card again against any other). Then the
      actual game starts according to the following rules:</p>

    <p>It starts the player needingt to get 9 ticks.</p>

    <p>Each turn can be started by any colour. Then you must stick with this
      colour; or, if a player doesn't have any card of this colour, play any
      colour (be it a trump or not).</p>

    <p>The played stapel wins the player having played the highest card (of
      the colour of the first played card or with the highest trump). The
      winner starts the next round.</p>

    <p>The difference between the made and the proposed ticks is important in
      the next round. The or those player having too much ticks can give this
      difference of any (usually bad) cards to those having too less ticks.
      Those people must return their heighest cards of these colours (or - if
      they shouldn't have cards with that colour - any (even bad) card).</p>

    <p>The next round starts the player which before needed to get 5 ticks.
      Now he must get 9 ticks.</p>

    <p>The game ends, if one persons gets more than 5 ticks too much.</p>

    <p>The status line gives some information about what's going on!</p>

    <h2>Note to the layout</h2>
    <p>After starting a game you have (from left to right and from top to
      bottom):</p>

    <ul>
      <li>A card (an ace) indicating the special colour.</li>
      <li>The cards of the human player. The won cards (above) become visible,
        if they are clicked into; right-clicking allows to sort them.</li>
      <li>The played pile.</li>
      <li>In the last line there are computer player 1 and 2</li>
    </ul>

